# Template and planning migration changes

Source: open-gsd/gsd-core c0b2a05d2f310adc0a1f35fd71fbc9f28f4e4977 (MIT).

This log records adaptations and conflict resolutions, not a shortened replacement
for upstream templates. Imported guidance must retain its full instructional text.

## 2026-09-13 — Scope and precedence

- The current request authorizes complete upstream templates, a separate .planning
  data directory, supporting reference reconciliation, and draft progress pushes.
- Earlier exclusions of milestone templates and phase-local PLAN naming are
  superseded where they conflict with this request. Optional templates do not
  create an adopting project or require using every artifact for every change.
- The phase runtime retains its no-automatic-merge boundary. The coordinator may
  merge this migration after review and checks under the user's explicit request.
- Primary has an existing uncommitted .ai/templates/CONTEXT.md edit. Preserve it
  throughout migration and final synchronization; all work uses assigned worktrees.

## 2026-09-13 — Local workflow and ownership adapters

- Move project data references from .ai to .planning; keep reusable rules,
  templates, source methods and Python runtime in .ai. Update relative links,
  explicit assigned read paths and document-ownership hook paths together.
- Adopt phase-local NN-CC-PLAN.md names instead of NN-CC-IMPLEMENT.md.
  Plans retain full upstream task/action/verify/done structure and must_haves.
- Link local discussion, research, preparation, execution and verification
  procedures to complete upstream templates and supporting methods. These
  references do not claim host registration of slash commands or named agents.
- Preserve existing exact ownership, resources, real argv checks, authorization,
  documentation coverage and revision evidence via additive runtime extensions.
- Keep the dependency-driven Python scheduler; upstream wave metadata remains
  useful for planning but does not force unrelated components through a barrier.
- Separate desired phase specifications (upstream spec.md) from verified current
  contracts (new CURRENT-SPEC.md). Expand the latter and ADR.md with complete
  authoring guidance, good/bad examples, consumers and handoffs.
- Document the explicitly authorized draft-progress path. The runtime's publish
  operation still requires verified work and never automatically merges.
- Add a portable local-link regression check for active workflow documentation;
  upstream imports have a separate provenance/reference audit. Historical
  migration records remain explicitly historical, not current instructions.
- Hook evidence: 10 document-ownership cases passed on Windows Git Bash.

## 2026-09-13 — Storage/runtime compatibility and documentation

- Project records move to .planning. The Python execution configuration remains
  YAML at .planning/config.yaml; GSD's complete config.json template is retained
  separately because its settings are not implemented by this Python adapter.
- The runtime accepts full GSD PLAN task/XML structure and requirement metadata;
  local additions carry authorization, checks, resource ownership and evidence.
  .ai/runtime/TEMPLATE-CONTRACT.md documents the producer/consumer contract.
- Old .ai planning records, IMPLEMENT artifacts and matching old-path checkpoints
  now produce actionable migration errors instead of silently appearing empty.
  Existing interrupted attempts must be inspected with their original runtime.
- All workflow/data-storage, onboarding, parallel execution, failure handling,
  verification, delivery and recovery guides are being reconciled against source.
- Both Windows Git Bash hook suites passed: 10 ownership cases and 49 worktree
  cases. These are hook results, not a claim that final migration checks passed.
# Complete template adaptation log

Source: https://github.com/open-gsd/gsd-core, revision `c0b2a05d2f310adc0a1f35fd71fbc9f28f4e4977`.

Generated by `tools/import_templates.py`; append this section to the
migration `changes.log`. Every template is listed, including unchanged
bodies. No example, counterexample, section, or guidance is abridged.

## Shared additive note

Every imported Markdown template and Markdown supporting file receives
the following separated local note after its complete upstream body.
Non-Markdown files receive only reversible namespace/reference substitutions
where needed, without an appended note; config.json remains byte-preserved.
The note resolves
local execution authority without deleting upstream system descriptions.

````text
<!-- LOCAL-ADOPTION:START -->
## Local adoption — read before using this source

This complete authoring guide retains its source content, examples, and methods.
Only recorded namespace/reference substitutions and explicit local conflict
corrections have been made. Source attribution and exact original hashes are
isolated in `.ai/library/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md` and `PROVENANCE.json`.

Read `.ai/library/README.md` for the local producer/consumer mapping and execution
boundary, `.ai/references/template-adaptation.md` for local conflict decisions,
and `.ai/runtime/TEMPLATE-CONTRACT.md` for additive local artifact
fields. Project records live in `.planning/`; reusable guidance lives in `.ai/`.
The active lifecycle uses `.ai/commands/` and `.ai/runtime/phase.py` with
`.planning/config.yaml`. The retained `config.json`, `/workflow:*` commands, tool
names, hooks, and Node CLI examples describe supporting source capabilities;
this import does not install or activate them. Source catalog pointers in examples
identify provenance, not executable command arguments. Retained specialty workflows are full source
guidance for explicit future integration, not promises of installed features.
Local rules, assigned worktrees, recorded authorization, runtime ownership and
verification safeguards govern execution. The local runtime never merges.
<!-- LOCAL-ADOPTION:END -->
````

